{% trace-scenario title="Did the prompt fix the problem?" prompt="A revised prompt improves one demo but increases refusals in production. What should you compare?" outcome="Compare the same task distribution by prompt version, outcome, failure class, latency, and cost instead of trusting one example." /%}

## At a glance

This guide is for **engineers and product teams changing prompts in production**. It helps you **turn prompt edits into reviewable experiments**. It does not replace application validation, permissions, or model-specific testing.

### The prompt contract

Use **Task → Context → Constraints → Output → Evaluation**. Each part should be explicit enough that another person can identify a failure without guessing what the prompt intended.

## Start with the task contract

Name the user outcome, inputs, allowed actions, refusal conditions, and output shape. Remove instructions that do not affect a decision. When a prompt depends on retrieved content or tool results, label those inputs as data and keep the trust boundary clear.

## Make outputs testable

For downstream code, prefer a schema with validation rather than parsing prose. [Structured outputs for AI agents](/blog/structured-outputs-ai-agents) covers the boundary between model suggestions and application-controlled actions. Keep validation outside the model and return a safe error when the output is incomplete.

```text
Task: classify the support request
Allowed labels: billing | access | technical | other
Output: one JSON object with label and confidence
If evidence is insufficient: label=other and explain why
```

This is an illustrative contract, not a guaranteed model behavior. Test malformed JSON, ambiguous inputs, long context, conflicting instructions, and empty fields.

## Version every meaningful change

Store prompt version, model, relevant configuration, evaluator version, and task cohort with each run. The [prompt versioning guide](/blog/prompt-versioning-production) explains why a prompt diff without outcome evidence is not a release record.

## Evaluate before rollout

Use a fixed regression set plus fresh production examples. Compare quality, refusal rate, tool-call correctness, latency, cost, and safety. Roll out to a narrow cohort with a stop condition and preserve the prior version for rollback.

## Release checklist

- Write the task, boundary, output, and refusal contract.
- Validate outputs in application code.
- Store prompt and model versions with every evaluation.
- Test adversarial, ambiguous, empty, and long inputs.
- Define the rollback threshold before the rollout.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Is there one perfect prompt?" answer="No. Prompt quality depends on the task distribution, model, tools, context, and outcome definition. Treat prompts as versioned system components and evaluate them with representative cases." /%}
{% faq-item question="Should prompts include chain-of-thought instructions?" answer="Focus on the output contract and evidence your application needs. Do not depend on hidden reasoning text as an audit record; capture structured decisions and tool actions instead." /%}
{% faq-item question="How often should prompts be reviewed?" answer="Review them whenever the task, model, tools, data, policy, or observed failure distribution changes. Re-run the regression set and inspect representative production traces." /%}

